<h2>Introduction to Liquid Volume</h2>
<p>Warm-up: source bowl and jar have different shapes. What do you notice/wonder? Which can hold more is a question for MEASUREMENT, not appearance alone. Liquid volume is how much space liquid takes up.</p>
<h3>Activity1: A and B, one common unit</h3><p>Teacher supplies two different-shape containers A/B and ONE small unit scoop. In groups of FOUR, estimate each vessel in units and verify by filling with the SAME small scoop.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Source real container</th><th>Too-low estimate</th><th>About-right</th><th>Too-high</th><th>Measured unit fills</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>A</th><td>______</td><td>______</td><td>______</td><td>______</td></tr><tr><th>B</th><td>______</td><td>______</td><td>______</td><td>______</td></tr>
</tbody></table><p>Discuss which real container has GREATER capacity and HOW same-unit fills justify it. Neither A nor B has one original fixed numeric value.</p>
<h3>Activity2: Liquid Volume in Liters (L)</h3>
<p>The class uses a one-liter bottle or cup, an initially EMPTY transparent vessel and a dry-erase marker. Estimate too-low/about-right/too-high liter capacity, then POUR and LABEL the ACTUAL water level after each complete 1L addition (0L at empty bottom;1L;2L;...).</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Estimates</th><th>Class physically observed result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Too low:_____; about right:_____; too high:_____</td><td>Actual counted full liters:_____ (+ any partial)</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The teacher's nine-liter example is not an answer key for the group's own vessel. Talk about why accumulating1L levels resembles an inch ruler with increasing marks. Original publisher photographs/video and gated Cool-down have not been reproduced. <a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-6/lesson-7/lesson.html">Original 2021 teacher source</a>.</p></section>